      "id": "theverge-2026-08-13-apple-and-epic-argue-over-how-much-apple-should-get-from-pur",
      "title": "Apple and Epic argue over how much Apple should get from purchases made outside the App Store",
      "summary": "In a new filing in its long-running legal dispute with Epic Games, Apple has proposed a structure that would allow it to collect fees on digital purchases made via external links that don't use the company's in-app purchase system. Epic has already responded, saying that Apple's request is \"far outside of the bounds\" of the court's guidance.\nRight now, Apple cannot take a commission on external pu",
      "body": "In a new filing in its long-running legal dispute with Epic Games, Apple has proposed a structure that would allow it to collect fees on digital purchases made via external links that don’t use the company’s in-app purchase system. Epic has already responded, saying that Apple’s request is “far outside of the bounds” of the court’s guidance.\nRight now, Apple cannot take a commission on external purchases after California district court judge Yvonne Gonzalez Rogers ruled in April 2025 that the company “willfully” didn’t comply with her 2021 Epic Games v. Apple injunction. However, a Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals panel said that Apple should be able to charge a commission based on “necessary costs,” and Apple shared its proposal for fees in Thursday’s filing. (The Supreme Court has also said it will hear arguments about whether Apple willfully violated the April 2025 ruling.)\nUnder Apple’s proposal, it would collect:\n* 15 percent of fees from purchases in “standard apps”\n* 10 percent of fees for apps in the Video Partner Program, the News Partner Program, the Mini Apps Partner Program, and subscription renewals\n* 5 percent of fees from apps in the Small Business Program\nHowever, Apple says its “necessary costs” to allow external purchases, as defined by the Ninth Circuit, “would be essentially zero.” The company argues that, based on an “expert analysis,” at the proposed rates, “it appears that large numbers of U.S. developers collectively accounting for the lion’s share of App Store revenue will be able to link out profitably.” Apple also says the rates would let it recoup “at least some compensation” for its tools, technologies, and services it provides to developers.\nHere’s Epic’s statement in reply to the filing:\n> Apple’s filing is in, and Apple admitted that under the Ninth Circuit’s definition of “necessary costs” they would charge 0% for purchases made via linkouts to the web.\n> Apple proposed linkout fees of 15% for standard apps and 5% for Small Business Program apps. Epic believes these fees are far outside of the bounds of the Ninth Circuit’s guidance on permissible fees, and we have roughly 60 days to file our opposition supported by expert witnesses.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Apple",
        "News",
        "Policy",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Jay Peters",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-13T23:13:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/04/STK468_APPLE_ANTITRUST_CVIRGINIA_F.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/979967/apple-epic-games-external-links-fees-filing",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
