      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-12-ai-coding-startup-cognition-reportedly-already-in-talks-to-r",
      "title": "AI coding startup Cognition reportedly already in talks to raise at $40B valuation",
      "summary": "Cognition may be looking to raise another mega round just a few months after raising $1 billion at a $26 billion valuation.",
      "body": "11:19 AM PDT · August 12, 2026\nCognition, maker of the AI coding agent Devin, is reportedly already talking to investors about raising another funding round with a big leap in valuation, after it raised $1 billion at a $26 billon valuation in May.\nThis new round could deliver at least a $40 billion valuation based on achieving a $1 billion annualized revenue run rate, according to sources cited by Bloomberg.\nThree months ago, when it announced its last raise, Cognition’s Scott Wu confirmed to TechCrunch that it had achieved a $492 million annualized revenue run rate and that enterprises were growing their usage of Devin by 50% month-over-month for the past six months.\nWu, a famed wunderkind programmer himself, also told TechCrunch that Devin is not being sold as a human replacement. The agent is often tasked with doing long-tail grunt-work that many programmers dislike, such as bringing old software up to date or moving applications off one platform and onto another. This could account for much of its growing popularity among enterprises that have adopted it. The company says its customers include Mercedes-Benz, NASA, and Goldman Sachs.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Julie Bort",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-12T18:19:12.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Scott-wu-headshot-e1780070238342.png?resize=1200,1004",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/12/ai-coding-startup-cognition-reportedly-already-in-talks-to-raise-at-40b-valuation/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-08-12-your-key-to-success-isn-t-more-luck-or-hard-work",
      "title": "Your Key to Success Isn't More Luck or Hard Work",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://julienreszka.com/blog/your-key-to-success-isn-t-more-luck-or-hard-work/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49276534\nPoints: 3\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "Hard work is grinding and only partly reliable. Luck is random by definition. Research on who you know is far more specific than most networking advice.Three things supposedly decide whether you succeed: how hard you work, how lucky you get, and who you know. Hard work gets treated as the real answer, the one thing fully within your control, the reason someone made it and someone else did not. The best evidence available says that is not the deciding factor. Who you know is.\nRaj Chetty and a team of economists used 21 billion Facebook friendships from 72 million Americans to measure economic connectedness, the share of a person's friends who earn more than they do. Children who grow up with high economic connectedness earn 20 percent more as adults on average than otherwise similar children who do not, a gap equal to growing up in a family making 47,000 dollars a year instead of 27,000. It was the single strongest predictor of upward mobility they found, ahead of school quality, segregation, and poverty rates.\nPeople near the bottom of the income ladder have almost no rich friends. Fewer than 2 percent of their friends are in the top 10 percent of earners.\nPeople at the top cluster with each other instead: 34 percent of their friends come from that same top 10 percent.\nNetwork cohesion and high rates of volunteering, the kind of social capital most people actually try to build, showed no meaningful link to upward mobility.\nThe same pattern shows up in how people find jobs, not just how much they eventually earn. A classic 1973 survey found that 56 percent of men got their job through a contact they saw only occasionally, not a close friend. A 2022 randomized experiment on 20 million LinkedIn users confirmed the effect is causal: moderately weak ties, people you know a little but not well, created more job mobility than close ones.\nHard work and luck are both real, but neither is a lever you can pull whenever you want. The circle of people you know a little, not well, is a lever you can pull, the same way an algorithm pulled it for millions of LinkedIn users just by suggesting the right people to them. Most people spend a decade optimizing how hard they work and never spend an afternoon on who they know. The data says they have it backwards.\nBefore crediting hard work for your best opportunity, look at who actually got it for you. The people who move your career and your income are usually not your closest contacts or your longest hours. Build the layer of people you know a little, on purpose.\nFree to try, no login needed. Results saved locally in your browser.\nDescribe what you do and your own interests, and find people who align. Find your people",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "julienreszka",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-12T18:17:03.000Z",
