    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-08-12-google-s-pixel-11-series-pairs-a-little-new-hardware-with-a-",
      "title": "Google’s Pixel 11 series pairs a little new hardware with a lot of new software",
      "summary": "When I first picked up the Pixel 11 Pro this week, it was clear to me that this was one of those refinement years - at least when it comes to hardware. Aesthetically, the phones are as beautiful as ever, with Google's signature camera bar and bright, colorful backing glass. There are small updates, like a single piece of glass covering the camera bar and the all-new HiLight ambient LED on the Pros",
      "body": "When I first picked up the Pixel 11 Pro this week, it was clear to me that this was one of those refinement years — at least when it comes to hardware. Aesthetically, the phones are as beautiful as ever, with Google’s signature camera bar and bright, colorful backing glass. There are small updates, like a single piece of glass covering the camera bar and the all-new HiLight ambient LED on the Pros. But this year, the Pixel is all about quality of life and software improvements.\nAfter weeks of teasers, Google has finally formally announced the Pixel 11, Pixel 11 Pro, Pixel 11 Pro XL, and Pixel 11 Pro Fold. The phones are all shipping next week, on August 20th, and Google is holding a splashy event the evening of August 12th to show off the devices. Ahead of that, we had a chance to take a first look at everything that’s coming up.\n[Image: The standard Pixel 11 gets some bold colors this year. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Google-Pixel-11-Hot-pink.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nLet’s start with the bad news up front: All of these phones are $100 more expensive than last year’s. Google has killed off the 128GB base storage option this year, which means the Pixel 11 now starts at $899 for a 256GB model. All storage tiers still come with 12GB of RAM, but the higher entry price is no doubt driven by RAMageddon, which has been increasing costs across the industry.\nIf you can look past the price, you’ll find a refined version of last year’s phone that looks and feels a bit more premium. The camera bar is now made of a singular piece of glass and is over 40 percent thinner. If you use it with one of Google’s cases, the bar will become completely flush. (This gives you three different camera bar thickness options on the Pixel line now, which only get thicker the more premium you go.)\n[Image: The camera bump difference between the Pixel 11 and Pixel 11 Pro. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Google-Pixel-11-vs-Pro-camera-bump-comparison.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThe cameras have been upgraded, too. On the Pixel 11, the 48MP wide is quoted with 56 percent more light gathering capability from its new 1/1.56-inch sensor, and the 5x telephoto lens can now digitally zoom up to 30x, which Google says is enabled by the new image signal processor in the Tensor G6 chipset. And considering the new Tensor G6 chip is said to be up to 20 percent more efficient, Google is quoting up to 30-plus hours of battery life across the lineup.\nPerhaps the most interesting update to me is the new Camera Looks feature. You can think of this similarly to the film recipes on Fujifilm cameras, presenting users with stylized presets that can be customized to their liking.&nbsp;\nWhat makes Camera Looks so interesting, though, is instead of just being a filter on top of the preprocessed Pixel photo, the phone will process the photo completely differently at the sensor level depending on which settings you choose, potentially using less frame stacking and sharpening to deliver the photo you actually want. During my briefing at least, this really delivered. The Natural mode does an amazing job of lowering the sharpness and contrast and making the photos look much more true to life, and I’m excited to take it for a proper spin later. If you want to learn more about this, check out my dedicated article where I spoke with Pixel camera lead Isaac Reynolds.\nAnother new feature inside the camera is Magic Capture. This is a camera mode alongside the photo and video modes that creates a sort of hybrid mashup of the two, where the phone will automatically pick out short clips and images you can share. During my hands-on time this feature didn’t work great, only giving me one small clip and one photo from a roughly 15-second recording, but I believe it’s meant for longer clips at things like kids’ birthday parties. We’ll have to test this in the field once we have more time with the device.\nGoogle is also adding a new Creator Suite inside the camera, which gives you access to tools like social media framing guides, speech enhancement, a teleprompter, and automatic folder structures for video. I was surprised to see this so deeply integrated into the camera itself, and it’s clear Google is going after a new audience and hoping to make the Pixel the best phone for small creators.\nThe phones also all feature Qi2.2 — an upgrade from Qi2 last year. Google says this offers 25 percent faster wireless charging of up to 25W, an upgrade from the 15W max the Pixel 10 offered. However, many users reported seeing a maximum wireless charge rate of only 5W with the Pixel 10 when using older chargers, so hopefully the problem is fixed this year.\nThe Pixel 11 will come in purple, green, black, and an extremely hot magenta.\nPixel 11 Pro and Pro XL\n[Image: The Pixel 11 Pro in blue. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Google-Pixel-11-Pro-back-of-phone-in-hand.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThe 11 Pro and Pro XL are where things get a little more interesting this year. Like the standard Pixel 11, they start at 256GB of storage and 12GB of RAM (down from 16GB last year), which starts at $1,099. If you’re a power user, you can still get 16GB of RAM if you buy the 512GB or 1TB models.\nYou’re definitely getting more with the Pro models this year, though. The display on the Pixel 11 Pro gets up to a super bright 3,600 nits, which Google says is the brightest display ever on a smartphone. This isn’t a massive difference from the 3,300-nit peak brightness from last year, but it’s something for your money.\nThe main camera sensor on the Pixel 11 Pro has been improved, with a new 50MP model that Google says uses less power and should perform better in the 1x-to-5x zoom range. There’s also a new 48MP telephoto lens with a larger 1/1.95-inch sensor that delivers a quoted 30 percent improvement in light sensitivity. And just like the standard Pixel 11, the 11 Pro has a further zoom range, now extending up to 120x, which Google says is enabled by the ISP in Tensor G6.\nOn the Pro phones, Google has also substantially sped up Night Sight, offering up to 4.5x faster low-light image capture, though Google tells me on average it’s about 3x faster than last year. This is still a pretty substantial improvement, and Reynolds told me the idea was to optimize Night Sight for handheld capture. “You can still capture for up to four minutes if you want all the light,” he tells me, “but it’s really optimized for those awkward moments where you have to tell someone to hold still for a little too long.”\n[Image: The HiLight LED replaces the old temperature sensor. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Google-Pixel-11-Pro-Pixel-Glow-Macro.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThe flashiest feature (pun intended) of the new Pro phones is the HiLight feature. Pixel 11 Pro and Pro Fold phones now feature an RGB LED ring where the temperature sensor used to be on the right side of the camera bar. When you’re talking to Gemini, the LED will glow blue as it listens and responds to you. Separately, you can set favorite contacts to glow when they call you through the default Phone app or WhatsApp. Google is marketing the feature as a way to be more present in your everyday life, but it doesn’t feel particularly useful to me yet considering its use is so limited. Google says more functionality is coming in the future, but right now, this doesn’t feel like a reason to buy the phone.\nThe Pixel 11 Pro and Pro XL will come in olive, light blue, and a striking orange-ish “canyon” color, all with glossy side rails. There’s also a black option, which this year has matte side rails, unlike all the other Pro devices.\n[Image: The Pixel 11 Pro Fold in olive. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Google-Pixel-11-Pro-Fold-closed-in-hands.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nFinally, there’s the Pixel 11 Pro Fold. Google is calling it the most durable Pixel Fold yet, with a new composite back material that Google says is nearly impossible to crack. This, alongside a thicker inner ultra-thin glass display, contributes to Google’s “3x more durable” claim compared to last year.\nThe inner bezels have also been reduced, making it Google’s thinnest Fold bezels ever, and the peak brightness matches the Pixel 11 Pro at 3,600 nits. The 11 Pro Fold is also inheriting the new 48MP main sensor from the Pixel 11 for a quoted 56 percent improved light sensitivity compared to last year, and it gets Qi2.2 for up to 20 percent faster wireless charging.\nThe 11 Pro Fold starts at 16GB of RAM standard with 256GB of storage, comes in olive and black, and starts at $1,899 — also a $100 price jump over last year.\nPhotography by David Imel / The Verge",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Gadgets",
        "Google",
        "Google Pixel",
        "Hands-on",
        "Mobile",
        "Phones",
        "Reviews",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "David Imel",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-12T14:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/Google-Pixel-11-Canyon-2.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.734771188106,100,78.530457623787",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/978013/google-pixel-11-series-hands-on-hardware-software",
      "readTimeMinutes": 7,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-12-messi-unsure-he-will-play-much-longer-after-father-s-death",
      "title": "Messi unsure he will play 'much longer' after father's death",
      "summary": "Lionel Messi says he is unsure how much longer he will continue playing football in an emotional tribute after his father's death.",
      "body": "Lionel Messi said he is unsure how much longer he will continue playing football as he posted an emotional tribute after his father's death.Messi's father Jorge was 68 when he died last week following a long illness.Jorge had been the Argentina forward's agent since he was 14 and watched him become one of the sport's greatest ever players.\"I don't know what I'm going to do without you,\" eight-time Ballon d'Or winner Messi wrote on social media.\"I don't know how to carry on. I used to just play football, and now I'm really not sure if I'll carry on doing it for much longer.\"You were by my side from the very beginning. We were so close to the end. Why didn't you hang in there just a little longer so we could finish this together?\"Messi captained Argentina as they won the World Cup in 2022 and led them to the 2026 World Cup final, which his side lost 1-0 to Spain, before returning to club side Inter Miami.He returned home with his family after Jorge passed away in a hospital in his hometown of Rosario, Argentina.A private funeral took place on Sunday at a cemetery in the town of Perez, on the outskirts of Rosario.\"Dad, I still can't believe you're gone,\" Messi continued. \"It hasn't sunk in, or rather, I don't want it to. \"It's so hard for me to imagine that I'll never see you again, that we'll never talk again. I know you were suffering and that this is for the best, but you left too soon. We still had so much left to enjoy together.\"During the World Cup, Messi was brought to tears after scoring the opening goal in Argentina's 3-0 win against Algeria because of a matter he said was \"completely unrelated to football\". A statement from his family later said Jorge was dealing with a \"health-related situation\".Messi added: \"You kept asking me to play in one last World Cup, and just days before it started, that's when you took a turn for the worse. \"It was the first time you weren't going to be at a tournament, but Mom kept telling me you'd get better and that you'd be well enough to travel. I kept telling you that we were going to make it to the final so you could come along.\"Every time a game ended, I'd wait for and miss your message. That's when I realised how bad the situation was. \"Even so, I couldn't stop thinking about going as far as possible, to give you time to watch a game. We made it to the final, and you couldn't be there. \"I wanted to win it so I could bring the trophy to you and show you a new one. I couldn't, my legs had nothing left. This time I tried to push past my physical limits, but I couldn't. I never managed to feel right.\"Messi made his sixth appearance at a World Cup as he captained Argentina at the 2026 tournament.He played eight games during the competition, scoring eight goals and registering four assists.Messi's family relocated to Spain in February 2001 after he was signed by Barcelona aged just 13 and, while his mother and three siblings soon moved back to Rosario, his father stayed with him.The forward went on to score a record 672 goals in 778 games for Barca, helping the club to win the Champions League four times and La Liga title on 10 occasions.He joined Paris St-Germain in August 2021 before moving to Inter Miami in July 2023, with his contract at the Major League Soccer side running until 2028.On the international stage, he has scored 125 goals in 207 appearances for Argentina and provided 46 assists. \"Of course, you never missed a single game,\" said Messi. \"How you suffered watching me play and how much you enjoyed it, even though you never gave me much praise.\"You were my dad, my friend and my mentor. You were always exactly the person you needed to be in every moment, and you were never wrong about anything. \"Despite a few disagreements or arguments, you were always right. In the end, things always turned out just as you said they would.\"I'm going to miss you so much, but you'll always be with me, especially in raising my children, because I teach and raise them just as you both did with me.\"Rest in peace, and watch over us from above just as you did here. Thank you for everything. \"I love you, Dad.\"Following Messi's emotional tribute, Portugal international Cristiano Ronaldo posted a message to his fellow forward.  \"A huge hug to you and yours in these hard times, Leo. Much strength,\" said the 41-year-old.Inter Miami co-owner and ex-England international Beckham said: \"We are with you and your family, Leo, always.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-12T13:48:23.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/a53f/live/2137b990-9656-11f1-9743-c91876572530.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/c78gqj2dqyno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-12-estonia-rejects-proposals-to-take-prisoners-from-uk-jails",
      "title": "Estonia rejects proposals to take prisoners from UK jails",
      "summary": "Estonia's justice minister rules out a deal with the UK after Reform and the Conservatives suggested renting prison space abroad.",
      "body": "The Estonian government has rejected the idea of taking prisoners from the UK to help ease the overcrowding crisis in British jails.Justice Minister Liisa Pakosta told the BBC Estonia would not rent jail cells to other countries while it was housing criminals from Sweden in its half-empty Tartu prison under a deal signed last week.The Conservatives and Reform UK have both suggested renting cells in foreign prisons, including in Estonia, to free up space and prevent the early releases of some offenders in England and Wales.The Labour government also explored the idea in 2024 but talks with Estonia came to nothing.Pakosta said Estonia had received no proposals to rent its prison space from the UK, adding that if one was to be made \"our answer would be unequivocal - in Margaret Thatcher's famous words: No. No. No.\"She added: \"Such a proposal would not merely be mistaken, misguided or unacceptable; it would deserve to be rejected outright.\"Thatcher memorably repeated \"no\" three times in 1990 speech, as she fiercely rejected calls for deeper political and economic integration of the European Community, which later evolved into the European Union.In 2024, the Labour government, when Sir Keir Starmer was prime minister, held talks with Estonia to discuss the Baltic state's policy to house some prisoners from abroad.Pakosta previously told the BBC she had met then-UK Justice Secretary Shabana Mahmood in Lithuania.Prime Minister Andy Burnham's government is planning to release thousands of prisoners early this October, as it scrambles to free up space in jails almost at 100% capacity.On Monday, Downing Street did not rule out renting space in foreign prisons, when asked by reporters if the government was considering doing so.A No 10 spokesperson said the government was focused on \"creating that extra capacity within the system domestically\".\"In terms of renting prison places abroad, it's just not a quick fix,\" the spokesperson added. \"It will require primary legislation underpinned by a complex international agreement which would take several years to agree.\"A government source told the BBC that renting foreign prison space would not help with the short-term crisis in jails.Burnham has ordered the justice secretary to come up with a plan to create more space in jails by September.He said the plan would be based on removing more foreign national offenders from prisons, releasing some offenders on indefinite sentences, and using space in women's jails for male inmates.Other parties have come up with their own ideas for dealing with overcrowding in prisons.At a news conference on Monday, Reform UK suggested 10,000 prison spaces could be freed up if all foreign criminals were sent abroad to serve their sentences.Reform UK leader Nigel Farage said his party was exploring options to transfer foreign criminals in the UK to prisons in other countries, including El Salvador and Kosovo.When asked if he had other countries in mind, Farage said: \"Yes there are Baltic state options as well.\"I mean, conversations could certainly be had with Lithuania, for example, or perhaps Estonia as well.\"A Reform UK spokesman said its policy was focused on El Salvador, Kosovo and \"possibly others if they are open to the idea\".In recent days, the Conservatives have also urged Burnham to revive their plan to rent prison cells overseas, a policy they introduced when the party was in government in 2023.The proposal aimed to mirror models used by Norway and Belgium in the Netherlands, where both countries rented cells to deal with overcrowding in their prisons.Conservative shadow home secretary Chris Philp said this was \"an idea that we legislated for in government but Labour subsequently did not pursue that\".\"That was a catastrophic mistake by the Labour government that should be urgently reversed.\"The Conservatives did not respond to requests to comment on Estonia's rejection of a prison rental deal with the UK.In a statement, Lithuania's Ministry of Justice said: \"There have been no discussions or consultations regarding the possibility of accepting the transfer of all Lithuanian citizens imprisoned in the United Kingdom\".A spokesperson said the Lithuanian government did not \"see any scope for entering into practical arrangements of this kind\".The Kosovo government, which has agreed to lease 300 of its jail cells to Denmark as part of a deal worth £170m (€200m), appeared more open to working with other countries on prisoner transfers.A Kosovo government spokesperson said: \"We value the willingness of partner countries to initiate and advance new co-operation initiatives that serve common interests, mutual priorities, and the strengthening of international justice mechanisms\".But the spokesperson stressed Kosovo would focus on enacting its deal with Denmark \"before entering into any new agreement of a similar nature\".The BBC has previously been told both former Conservative and Labour ministers independently came to the view that renting prison space abroad would be expensive.Sweden has agreed to pay up to £52m (€61m) a year to Estonia if the Baltic nation does house 600 of its offenders at the Tartu prison.Estonia said from the 301st inmate onwards, Sweden would pay an additional £7,254 (€8,500) per prisoner, per month.The average annual cost per prisoner in a public-sector prison in England and Wales was £60,018 in 2024/25, according to Ministry of Justice data.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-12T13:47:12.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/7f0c/live/0d341b90-9643-11f1-8414-97750c5a4202.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cly5pj0qyd4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-12-lucy-davis-urges-women-to-check-their-breasts-for-cancer-her",
      "title": "Lucy Davis urges women to check their breasts for cancer - here's how",
      "summary": "The actress from The Office TV series says her breast lump was tiny and not a 'lump' as such; rather a kind of hard spot.",
      "body": "Image caption,  Lucy Davis as Dawn in The OfficeByMichelle RobertsDigital health editorSigns of breast cancer are not always a lump in the traditional sense, so what should you look for?Actress Lucy Davis, who played Dawn Tinsley in the original UK version of the popular TV series The Office, has revealed she has incurable breast cancer.On Instagram, external, the 53-year-old said it had spread to her bones and it was too late for chemo.She added: \"The initial lump that I felt, wasn't a 'lump' as such; rather a kind of hard spot. Really tiny.\"Here's how to check yourself at home.What to look forThe NHS says to check your breasts, external or chest about once a month.That helps you learn what looks and feels normal for you, making it easier to notice any abnormal changes. Breasts can naturally look and feel a bit different at different phases of your menstrual cycle, and change a bit as you age. Instead, pay attention to:a lump or swelling in your breast, chest or armpita change in the skin of your breast, such as dimpling (it may look like orange peel) or redness, which may be harder to see on black or brown skina change in size or shape of one or both breasts, or either side of your chest – it's common for breasts to be different sizes, but check for any changes that are not normal for younipple discharge (if you're not pregnant or breastfeeding), which may have blood in ita change in the shape or look of your nipple, such as it turning inwards (inverted nipple) or a rash appearing on it (it may look like eczema)sores or ulcers on your chestHow to check your breastsLook at your breasts or chest in a mirror to look for any changes. Start with your arms by your sides and then raise them up.Feel around each breast or side of your chest in a circular motion all the way up to your collarbone and under each armpit. Use a mix of light and firmer pressure. Do not press so hard it hurts.Feel around and over each nipple.When should I see a doctor?See your GP if:you have a lump or swelling in your breast, chest or armpityou have any changes in your breasts, chest or nipples that are not normal for youyou have pain in your breast or armpit that does not go awayMore on this story",
