      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-09-anthropic-is-turning-claude-code-s-auto-mode-on-by-default",
      "title": "Anthropic is turning Claude Code’s auto mode on by default",
      "summary": "Programming with Claude Code will soon require even less human oversight.",
      "body": "12:20 PM PDT · August 9, 2026\nImage Credits:Samuel Boivin/NurPhoto / Getty Images\nProgramming with Claude Code will soon require even less human oversight, as Anthropic says it’s making auto mode the default for Pro, Max, and Team accounts, starting on August 14.\nThe company unveiled a test version of auto mode in March, pitching it as a way to balance speed and control. As Anthropic explained in its announcement on Friday, when Claude Code is in auto mode, instead of presenting prompts asking for human approval at each step, it will proceed unless an action is determined to be “irreversible, destructive, or aimed outside your environment.”\nAnthropic also said that in testing, auto mode proved safer than manual review — in a study with 1,053 paid testers, auto mode caught 89% of harmful actions, while human review only caught 13.6%. (Perhaps that’s because “manual review can become habitual: users approve 97% of permission prompts in Claude Code.”)\nIn a post on X, Claude Code head Boris Cherny said, “The team and I use Auto mode exclusively, and have been for many months. I couldn’t imagine going back to permission prompts!”\nThe company also said it’s been adding new safety features like prompt injection screening and customizable hard deny rules to prevent things like data exfiltration.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Anthony Ha",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-09T19:20:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/GettyImages-2269811684.jpg?w=1024",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/09/anthropic-is-turning-claude-codes-auto-mode-on-by-default/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-08-09-how-i-use-llms-to-learn-complex-topics",
      "title": "How I use LLMs to learn complex topics",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://laurentiugabriel.github.io/blog/articles/how-i-use-llms-to-learn/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49234675\nPoints: 692\n# Comments: 442",
      "body": "Many engineers I know use generative AI for many functions, like building PoCs, internal tools or dashboards, or even learning new stuff. I personally find the style used by LLMs to explain things difficult to follow. It's just too simplistic and depending on the number of emojis used, a bit annoying too.\nWhile I was analyzing new AI bottlenecks that might slow down data center buildup, I realized there are many aspects of chip production that I do not know. Surfing the web, I asked myself what if there would be a game to get you through the process of building a chip at a fab? For sure learning this way will stick, since you can map concepts with objects within the game. This is when I decided to try it, and it actually turned out really well.\nInstead of just asking  AI to explain a topic, I use the following flow:\nIn plan mode (using CC, or OpenCode) I ask a model to build the foundational knowledge for X topic.\nI ask it to review the accuracy of the knowledge base it built in the previous step.\nI proceed asking it to build a simulation of that topic in a low-poly, Rollercoaster Tycoon-like animation. I add some UX elements as well, like the page needs to be visible on both large and small screens, have controls to stop the flow whenever I want etc.\nI then push it to a new repo and enable GitHub Pages for it.\nWhat you get is a beautiful animation that is 100% accurate and free of hallucinations. For me, this method works a lot better than just reading endless materials that I find on Google, or trying to digest a bulleted list that is spat by a language model.\nI've done this specifically for learning chip building and launch it under this website: ChipTycoon. You get to follow a cart from the moment when sand is collected, to the moment when a chip is finalized and delivered to a data center.\nVisually, you can follow the cart and see how it changes too. Since it's low-poly, the details might be missing, but it's still a good indicator for showing how the product changes once it goes through the many steps required in the manufacturing process.\nHow to improve it further\nLet's say that the low-poly design requires to much immagination to actually visualize what happened to the quartz sand pile after it left the furnace. To transform this into a more realistic representation, you can use my skill for transforming pictures into 3d objects, and map the resulting objects to your simulation. This way you get more accurate design.\nAlso, you can add challenges to your simulation too. Trying to answer questions about a previous step in the chip manufacturing process will help you retain the knowledge tremendously. Add intuitive puzzles too that will help you learn even better.\nCheck out what other pages I created:\nHow rocket engines are made\nHow F1 engines are built\nHow an EUV machine is built",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "laurentiurad",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-09T19:16:49.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://laurentiugabriel.github.io/blog/articles/how-i-use-llms-to-learn/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-09-defiant-hodgkinson-says-home-european-title-would-mean-as-mu",
      "title": "Defiant Hodgkinson says home European title would mean as much as Olympic gold",
      "summary": "Keely Hodgkinson will seek to prove she is still number one following a \"tricky\" outdoor season when she faces world-class rivals for European 800m gold in Birmingham.",
      "body": "Keely Hodgkinson will seek to prove she is still number one after a \"tricky\" outdoor season when she comes up against world-class rivals in pursuit of a third consecutive European 800m title in Birmingham.Olympic champion Hodgkinson, 24, has had much to celebrate in 2026, including breaking the long-standing indoor 800m world record, winning her first world title and improving her British record outdoors.However, she was forced to settle for second behind Audrey Werro in achieving the latter at the Stockholm Diamond League, after the 22-year-old Swiss ran the fastest time since the outdoor world record was set 43 years ago. While Werro has gone even faster since, a muscle \"twinge\" forced Hodgkinson's withdrawal from the 400m final at the British Championships in June and she finished second to world champion Lilian Odira in Eugene, where she competed with her knees heavily bandaged after a nasty fall in training.Add in the presence of the ever-improving Femke Broeders-Bol - the Dutch two-time world 400m hurdles champion who has chosen to switch events this season - and the women's 800m on Friday night has become the must-watch event of the championships.\"I feel in a good place, obviously there was a tough patch in June and July, but I'm happy, I'm excited to compete and I just think it'll bring out the best of me,\" Hodgkinson said.On her previous meeting with Werro, she added: \"I think people [are] trying to make me feel like it was a failure and it really wasn't. \"It was a British record, my fastest time ever, and I'm going to remind myself of that because I'm proud of that. \"I had a tricky few weeks but, honestly, I just think it's really good that Audrey has come on really well and Femke is running well. It's going to be a really good race. Let's see what happens.\"Hodgkinson returned to winning ways by beating Broeders-Bol at the London Diamond League last month and has since fine-tuned her preparations in Portugal with her team.She will hope to once again feed off the energy of a home crowd in Birmingham, and says achieving a gold medal on British soil would be \"incredibly special\" and \"mean just as much to me as winning [Olympic gold] in Paris\".A medal of any colour would be her 12th on the international stage in just six seasons, while gold would represent a fifth European title overall since her first in 2021.\"It's really special. Winning is very important to me this year because it'd be my fifth European title indoors and out, and that is five years of work,\" says Hodgkinson.\"That is five years turning up to every championships and winning. It's really hard to stay that consistent.\"After improving to one minute 53.80 seconds in June, Werro's personal best is more than half a second faster than Hodgkinson's 1:54.33, while Broeders-Bol clocked 1:55.60 behind the Swiss athlete's second sub-1:54 clocking in Paris.Hodgkinson does, however, have superior championship experience compared to Werro, who clinched her first senior international medal by taking world indoor silver behind the Briton in March.Asked how much she has spoken with coaches Trevor Painter and Jenny Meadows about how her opponents might try to beat her, Hodgkinson said: \"We've spoken about it a little bit. \"Ultimately I'll just focus on what I can do best and control my race.\"I don't know what anyone else is going to do, but I think it is going to be the race of the championship. It's going to be really fun.\"",
